{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"get-out-of-debt-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Get out of debt","description":"Takes someone from debt worry to a working plan in five checked steps - every debt listed, priorities protected, a budget, the right route out, and creditor letters with review dates.","category":"debt","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","ship","maintain"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["table","plan","message","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"basic","tags":["priority-debts","debt-plan","creditor-letters","money-worries"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-debt-payoff","negotiate-with-creditor","build-tight-budget","compare-debt-relief-options","prepare-for-debt-advice-appointment"],"personas":["personal-finance-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"debts","description":"Everything you owe that you know of: who to, what type, balance, rate, monthly payment, whether you are behind, and any letters. Rough figures are fine. Leave out account numbers.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"money","description":"Household take-home income, pay dates, and regular costs. Rough figures are fine.","type":"text"},{"name":"country","description":"Country and region, because priority debts, protections and free advice services differ.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"debt-list","file":"steps/01-debt-list.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/01-debt-list.md"},{"id":"budget","file":"steps/02-budget.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/02-budget.md"},{"id":"route","file":"steps/03-route.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/03-route.md"},{"id":"letters","file":"steps/04-letters.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/04-letters.md"},{"id":"keep-going","file":"steps/05-keep-going.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/05-keep-going.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Walks one household out of debt the way a free debt adviser would: first get every debt on one page and sort them by what happens if they go unpaid, then build a budget that protects essentials, then choose the route that fits (a payoff plan, arrangements with creditors, or formal options to take to an adviser), then write to creditors, then set up the routine that keeps the plan working. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse the approved figures.\n\n<debts>\n{{debts}}\n</debts>\n{{#money}}\n\n<money>\n{{money}}\n</money>\n{{/money}}\n{{#country}}\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n{{/country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n\nRules for every step:\n- From the first step, say that free, non-profit debt advice services exist in most countries, can check everything here and often deal with creditors for free; explain how to find one in general terms and name one only if confident it exists.\n- Use only figures the person gave or confirmed; mark gaps as [X] with a question. Show the arithmetic.\n- Priority debts (home, energy and water, council or local taxes, court fines, child support, tax, anything that risks the home, liberty, supply or essential goods) come before unsecured debts, whoever is chasing hardest.\n- If court papers, enforcement, eviction, repossession or disconnection appear at any point, stop and say to get free advice now and not to miss deadlines.\n- Do not recommend specific lenders, consolidation products or paid debt firms. Never suggest borrowing more at high cost to pay debts.\n- If no country is given, ask once in step 1 and keep country-specific points general until it is known.\n- Shame-free, calm and practical throughout.","steps":[{"id":"debt-list","file":"steps/01-debt-list.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/01-debt-list.md","text":"# Step 1: Debt list\n\n1. Put every debt in one table: creditor type, what it is, balance, interest rate, contractual payment, arrears, and the latest letter or action. Ask about common forgotten debts (overdrafts, buy now pay later, family loans, tax, phone contracts).\n2. Mark each debt priority or non-priority with the reason (what could happen if unpaid), and flag anything urgent (court, enforcement, disconnection, eviction, repossession).\n3. Flag any debt the person does not recognise or disputes, to be checked before any payment.\n4. Totals: priority arrears, non-priority balances, and contractual payments per month.\n\nSections: Urgent, Debt table, Priority versus non-priority, Debts to check, Totals, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"budget","file":"steps/02-budget.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/02-budget.md","text":"# Step 2: Budget\n\n1. Build a monthly income and spending budget from the money details, converting weekly and yearly items to monthly; check that every income the household may be entitled to has been considered, with benefits or credits marked to verify locally.\n2. Protect essentials and current priority bills first, then show the amount left for priority arrears and then for non-priority debts.\n3. Compare that amount with the contractual payments from step 1 and say plainly whether there is a surplus, a squeeze or a shortfall.\n4. Suggest realistic savings only where they do not harm health, work or children.\n\nSections: Income, Essentials, Priority payments, Available for other debts, Gap, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"route","file":"steps/03-route.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/03-route.md","text":"# Step 3: Route\n\n1. Based on the gap from step 2, describe the routes that fit: if all contractual payments are affordable, a payoff order (avalanche or snowball) with dates; if not, arrangements with creditors (reduced payments shared pro rata, interest and charges frozen); if the gap is large or long-term, the formal options to discuss with a free debt adviser, described in general terms and marked to verify locally.\n2. For the chosen route, show the plan per debt: monthly amount, start date and expected end or review date.\n3. Note the catches of the route (credit record effects, interest resuming, arrangements lapsing if payments are missed).\n4. Say clearly when the situation needs an adviser's assessment before going further, and what to bring.\n\nSections: Routes that fit, The plan, Catches, When to see an adviser. Stop for approval."},{"id":"letters","file":"steps/04-letters.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/04-letters.md","text":"# Step 4: Letters\n\n1. Write one short letter or email per creditor that the route needs: the situation in two sentences, the offer from step 3, a request to freeze interest and charges and hold collection while it is considered, a copy of the budget offered, and a request for written confirmation. Use placeholders for names and references.\n2. For disputed or unrecognised debts, write a request for proof of the debt and the right to collect, with no offer.\n3. Give a call script for creditors who phone, with calm answers to pressure to pay more than the budget allows.\n4. List what to keep: copies, dates, names and reference numbers.\n\nSections: Letters, Proof requests, Call script, Record keeping. Stop for approval."},{"id":"keep-going","file":"steps/05-keep-going.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","artifact":"get-out-of-debt/05-keep-going.md","text":"# Step 5: Keep going\n\n1. Set up the routine: payment dates aligned to pay days, automatic payments for priority bills, a small buffer for irregular costs, and how to avoid new borrowing.\n2. Diary dates: when replies are due, when to chase, and a review every three months or when income changes.\n3. Warning signs to act on early (a missed arrangement payment, a new letter, a change in income) and what to do for each.\n4. A one-page summary of the plan to keep, plus the questions still open for a free debt adviser.\n\nSections: Routine, Diary dates, Warning signs, One-page plan, Questions for an adviser."}]}
